# direnv: make plain `cargo build` / `cargo run --example X` target the
# host on Apple Silicon macOS.
#
# `.cargo/config.toml` pins `[build] target = "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"`,
# and has to: without an explicit target, the bare-metal Linux rustflags
# (`-static`, `relocation-model=static`, …) would also apply to the
# `goish-macros` proc-macro build and break it. Cargo config cannot make
# that pin depend on the host, but `CARGO_BUILD_TARGET` overrides it —
# so it is set here, and only on the one host where the native target is
# a goish target. Everywhere else this file does nothing.
#
# Without it, `cargo run --example hello` on a Mac builds a static Linux
# ELF and fails at link time (Apple's `ld` rejects `--no-dynamic-linker`
# and friends). With it, the example builds as a Mach-O and runs
# natively. Only the examples in scripts/darwin_arm64_examples.txt are
# known to work on this target; see plan.md for the rest.
#
# Run `direnv allow` once to enable it.
if [ "$(uname -s)" = "Darwin" ] && [ "$(uname -m)" = "arm64" ]; then
  export CARGO_BUILD_TARGET=aarch64-apple-darwin
fi
